  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**\n   The objective is to minimize the maximum contextual overlap of a step function \\( h: [0, 2] \\to [0, 1] \\) with integral 1. This is a global optimization problem with nonconvex structure. We adopt an exponential smoothing of the max correlation to enable gradient-based optimization. At a high level, we minimize the following smooth approximation of the original objective:\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}(h, \\varepsilon) = \\frac{1}{\\varepsilon} \\log \\left( \\sum_{k \\in K} \\exp(\\varepsilon \\cdot c_k) \\right)\n   \\]\n   where:\n   - \\( c_k \\) is the overlap correlation at lag \\( k \\),\n   - \\( \\varepsilon > 0 \\) controls the smoothness ($\\varepsilon \\to 0$ approximates the sharp max).\n   This smooths the max by combining the e^penalty method (numerically stable) and log-sum-exp trick (gradient-friendly). We initialize with uniform or random samples, apply projection for constraints, and use exponential smoothing with lag options to escape local optima.\n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**\n   - **Variables**: Piecewise constant function approximated by \\( n \\)-point samples.\n   - **Objective**: Minimize \\( \\mathcal{F}(h, \\varepsilon) \\) (defined above).\n   - **Constraints**:\n     - \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) for all \\( i \\),\n     - \\( \\sum_{i} h[i] = n/2 \\) (ensuring \\( \\int h(x) dx = 1 \\)).\n   - **Representation**: Discrete \\( n \\)-dimensional vector, projected/optimized with gradient steps.\n   - **Smoothed Objective**: \\( \\mathcal{F}(h, \\varepsilon) \\) defined with lag indices \\( K \\) chosen from \\( \\{-200, \\ldots, 200\\} \\), ensuring numerical stability.\n   - **Gradient**: Computed using finite differencing on the objective, incorporating exponential weighting.\n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**\n   - **Initialization**:\n     - Accepts `seed=42` and `budget_s=1100`.\n     - Uses global `initial_h_values` if available, otherwise starts with uniform samples satisfying constraints.\n     - Parameters: \\( n \\) (number of points), \\( \\varepsilon \\) (smoothing parameter), \\( \\Delta x = 2.0/n \\) (spacings).\n   - **Core Optimization Loop**:\n     - Iteratively apply projected gradient descent to minimize \\( \\mathcal{F}(h, \\varepsilon) \\).\n     - Projection: Ensure \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) and adjust sum to \\( n/2 \\) via uniform redistribution.\n     - Gradient: Use central differencing on \\( \\mathcal{F}(h, \\varepsilon) \\) with exponential weighting.\n     - Adaptive learning rate \\( \\eta = \\eta_0 \\exp(\\alpha \\cdot \\text{iterations}) \\) (controls stability).\n   - **Lag Management**:\n     - Set \\( K \\) to \\( \\{-200, \\ldots, 200\\} \\).\n     - Lower-epsilon phase (large \\( \\varepsilon \\) for slow convergence) transitions to high-epsilon phase (dense focusing).\n   - **Termination**:\n     - Track time budget (wall-clock limit).\n     - Accept best solution if plateau detected over iterations.\n\n4. **Search Choices and Variations**\n   - **Base Configuration**: \\( n = 500 \\), \\( \\varepsilon = 1.0 \\), \\( \\text{num\\_ite} = 2000 \\).\n   - **Adaptive Lag Focus**: Progressive tightening of \\( K \\) from \\( \\{-50, \\ldots, 50\\} \\) to \\( \\{-200, \\ldots, 200\\} \\).\n   - **Gradient Start**: Smooth parameter \\( \\varepsilon \\) initialized at 1.0, decreasing logarithmically.\n   - **Automatic Jump**: If optimization stalls, increase \\( n \\) by 100% and restart.\n\n5. **Compute Budget and Robustness**\n   - **Iteration Time**: Each \\( \\mathcal{F} \\) evaluation \\( O(n^2) \\) per candidate.\n   - **Budget Splitting**: 80% to optimization, 20% to adaptive resource increases.\n   - **Emergency Shutdown**: Time check at each iteration; peaceful exit if no improvement for \\( \\tau \\) iterations.\n   - **Fallback**: If initialization succeeds, maintain parent for consistent class.\n\n6. **Validation and Return Contract**\n   - **Feasibility**: After projection, ensure \\( \\sum_{i} h[i] \\approx n/2 \\) (within tolerance).\n   - **Objective Value**\n```\n\n\\mathcal{F}(h, \\varepsilon) \\)\n\n  - **Measure and Evaluate**\uff1a Compute score via correlation to verify.\n  - **Return**: (h_values, score, n_points, epsilon) for score interpretation.\n\nNo penalties; objective is sharply defined by integration.\n\nWe must re-run gradient-descent on discrete function \\( h \\) defined on \\( [0, 2] \\) and check feasibility.\n\nReturn directly if time exceeds budget, else maximize \\( h \\) through \\( K \\) scaling.\n\nNote: Heavily uses gradient descent and projection. Ensures fast convergence to tight solution through smooth approximation.\n\nThis ensures scalars are compared and computational time is minimized.\n\nReal code projections: Automatically done via statistical skips.\n\nLet actual implementation code include these details.",
